name: Telecom re-rating (tariff validation)
tagline: "Independent re-rating of rated usage against an effective-dated tariff - tariff-table hygiene, ambiguity verdicts and an unbilled preview, over a synthetic corpus"
description: "A re-rating / tariff-validation content pack (slices R0 to R3 of the plan: tariff versioning, ambiguity, causes, root causes and plan inference). Three feeds - the rated usage a billing system produced, the tariff as one row per version with its own effective dates and an optional PRIORITY, and the subscription history (the plan each subscriber was assigned, from and to an instant) - and four chained sql.template Jobs. rr_tariff_hygiene judges the tariff table itself (rows in force at once with the same rate or different rates, gaps, negative or missing rates, inverted intervals) and rr_rerate re-rates each call at its start, giving one verdict per call (single, agree, precedence, disagree, none), an expected charge or an expected RANGE, and separate under-charged and over-billed amounts that never net. A call that an ambiguous tariff could justify is explained, not leakage. rr_explain then tests each leakage or over-billing against counterfactual re-rating (a stale version, a lower-priority row, a version billed early, or the plan the subscriber's calls are billed at when the history assigns a different one), with a confidence that is exactness times support and every other fitting cause kept, and rr_root_cause groups the findings into root causes with revenue at risk (never including over-billing), a run rate and two KPIs. It compares nothing with the invoice and raises no Alert: those arrive in the next slices. Every threshold is configuration. This pack is an unbilled preview: it is not priced or ordered yet. Needs the Standard, Advanced or Preview edition."
minEdition: standard
icon: heroicons_outline:calculator
contents[7]: "3 Pipelines + schemas (rated usage, tariff versions, subscription history)", "4 chained sql.template Jobs (tariff hygiene, re-rating, explanation, root causes)", "7 Datasets (3 feeds, 4 Job-written)", "2 KPIs (revenue at risk, over-billed amount)", "Zero-row schema seeds", "Runbook skeleton", "Example SLA policies and Escalation Rules (inert)"
